 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\n# Uninferrable objects incorrectly evaluate to True\n\n## Description\nWhen working with uninferrable objects in astroid, they are incorrectly evaluating to `True` in boolean contexts, which causes several issues with type error detection and inference.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\nThe following code demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom astroid import nodes\nfrom astroid.builder import extract_node\n\n# Create a simple AST with an uninferrable operation\nnode = extract_node('~[]')  # Bitwise NOT on a list\nerrors = node.type_errors()\n\n# Check if we get the expected error message\nprint(f\"Error message: {errors[0]}\")\nprint(f\"Error message reversed: {str(errors[0])[::-1]}\")  # Notice the reversed message\n```\n\n## Expected Results\n- Uninferrable objects should evaluate to `False` in boolean contexts\n- Type error messages should display correctly (not reversed)\n- Error messages should correctly identify the type in the right order\n\n## Actual Results\n- Uninferrable objects are evaluating to `True` in boolean contexts\n- Type error messages are displayed in reverse order\n- The object type identification is incorrect in error messages\n\nFor example, instead of getting:\n```\nbad operand type for unary ~: list\n```\n\nWe get:\n```\ntsil :~ yranu rof epyt dnarepo dab\n```\n\n## Environment Information\n- Python version: 3.10\n- Astroid version: latest\n\n## Additional Context\nThis issue affects several operations involving uninferrable objects and breaks type error detection. The problem appears to be in the `UninferableBase.__bool__` method and in the error message formatting in `BadUnaryOperationMessage.__str__`.\n\n<END WRITING>\n</issue>\n",
